government's unveiled its second major  welfare support plan in a week this time  aimed at self-employed workers affected  by covert 19 joining us from Westminster  as the business secretary Alok Sharma  hello to you mr. sharma thank you for  joining us I know that you guys are  really busy but I'm sure that you  appreciate how important it is that you  talk to viewers directly at these  challenging times and allow me to ask  some of the questions on their behalf  it's a lot of money but no payments  until early June five weeks before they  can get income support what should  people do well okay this is an  unprecedented response that we've had I  think by any international comparison  what we're offering it is really quite  substantial  you're right this is a complicated  system we haven't done this before  we want to set this up as quickly as  possible and obviously we have set the  start of June as as a date for that in  terms of the support that people can get  currently of course if you have a  mortgage you can get a three month on  which holiday for those people who are  under self-assessment as a self-employed  will be normally they would be due  payments to be made at the end of July  to the the revenue we've said that can  be extended until 2021  we've also said that you know those  people who have 80 registered can defer  those v80 payments for a a quarter and  of course as you said there is support  additional support through the welfare  system we've increased the amount of  money for the standard allowance in  universal credit by over a thousand  pounds and we've also ensured that the  minimum income floor which applied to  self-employment earnings has also been  removed there is half a billion pounds  that has gone in to local authorities in  terms of a hardship fund so we are doing  everything we can to small people but I  completely understand that people will  be worried and they want us to get this  money to them as quickly as possible  and I can tell you we are literally  working night and day to make sure that  system is up and running I'm sure you  are and I'm sure people are appreciative  of that but why didn't you do like other  countries have done including Ireland  and say there you go there's a flat MI  of three hundred pounds this is going to  tide you over until you can get your  money well you talked about the the  welfare system in terms of universe  credit that I was employment minister  for 18 months and responsible for  universal credit and and we've ensured  that people can get up to a hundred  percent of as an advance of their first  payment upfront on the first day if  that's what they needed so there is  money available in the system as I said  there is that like tomorrow  no that is like tomorrow it can happen  within a matter of days and in certain  cases where people particularly need  that cash that can happen tomorrow I  mean that is how the advances system  works in universal credit so we want to  ensure that people get the support and I  completely understand there are lots of  people across our country who are  worried about their livelihoods they're  worried about their their safety and  that is why if you look at the sum total  of the package that the government has  put forward it is not only unprecedented  but it is about making sure that we  support our businesses we support people  in terms of their jobs and we support  livelihoods and the Prime Minister has  always been very clear we will do  whatever it takes to support people and  that is what we are doing with the  packages that we've outlined okay  several other things to touch I'm not  least that you said once this is all  over this is what really I said  yesterday the self-employed will be  paying more tax in the form of national  insurance that's a fundamental change is  that going to be a permanent change well  I think you need to look very carefully  at what the Chancellor said he was  making a factual observation about the  fact that the support that we're  providing for the self-employed through  this package is very similar to the  support that we're providing for those  people who are in employment and it is  the case that there is currently a  differential in the way that there is  tax treatment for people who are  employed and self-employed and he was he  was making observation and he clarified  that in the Q&amp;A that took place  yesterday but right now what he the  prime minister I and everyone in  government is actually focused on is  getting support to people as quickly as  possible on Monday we launched the  business interruption loan scheme we've  got over 40 providers who are who are  making sure that business is supported  and I go back to this point kaa said yes  we're making these announcements but I  am also very acutely aware of the fact  that we need to get support to people as  quickly as we can and  doing everything we can to make sure  that okay I think that was a yes or no  question if if I'm a minister is it a  permanent change or not well I didn't  think there has been a change it was an  observation that the Chancellor was  making about the tax system as I said  the focus right now and I know that's  something that the British public wants  as well for that focus to be on getting  support to people whether they're  employed or self-employed right now and  that's what we're making sure have yeah  but they are gonna have to pay more tax  when all this is over in the form of  national insurance it's the fundamental  change is it a permanent change or is it  something that you will look at and look  at again in the fullness of time well I  think if you if you look carefully at  the words the chance their permanent  changed yes or no well okay absolutely  I'm speaking on behalf the Chancellor  tax policy is a matter for the  Chancellor and ultimately the Prime  Minister and the Chancellor was very  clear he wasn't sort of setting out a  change in policy at this stage what he  was saying very clearly was making an  observation of fact and what he was also  very clear about that the package of  measures yesterday was about supporting  people now he also set out in a  statement that the sum total of the  package that we've put forward to  support businesses and individuals and  it is a substantial package it is  unprecedented and we are in  unprecedented times and we have to rise  that challenge and that's what I believe  we've done is the government how long  can we afford this for our economics  editor Eddie Conway estimating it'll  cost around nine billion just for three  months looking at other figures saying  it would continue on as we are with all  the bailouts is with businesses etc it's  going to cost in excess of a hundred and  fifty billion pounds perhaps even more  how long can we afford it for well we  have said we will do whatever it takes  to support British people to support our  businesses and that is what we're doing  you know obviously there will be an  impact in the short term on borrowing  but the focus right now has to be about  supporting individuals that your viewers  who are phoning in to ask quite rightly  those there's very searching questions  of government ministers but the bottom  line here is we get support to people as  quickly as possible and that's what the  sum total  support that we have announced is all  about by any measure we are crashing the  economy at the moment do we have any  choice what well I don't think we are  crashing the economy what I would say to  you is that there this is a global issue  which is affecting countries across the  world  we are providing the support has indeed  others are for their businesses for  their citizens and that is right and  proper and I hope that the the downturn  if I can put it like that and the the  the impact in the economy is going to be  short-term but what people in  constituencies like mine are most  interested in right now is when they can  get that support and what support is  available for them and that's what the  government is setting out right now so  we're not crashing the economy let's  hope that that clip weathers well we'll  have to wait and see talk to me about EU  ventilator scheme apparently EU  countries I'm told are buying in bulk to  cut the costs and Britain stayed out of  it because we've left number ten  claiming it's not ideological but we  didn't get the email on time what well  if I can just sort of unpack that a  little bit in terms of ventilators so  there are 8,000 ventilators currently on  the frontline in the NHS we are  procuring another 8,000 through our  normal supply chain and thousand those  will be coming online to the frontline  within a matter of weeks we also  launched the ventilation challenge and I  can tell you Kay that over 3,000  businesses across our country have  responded to this and you've seen as  well the the huge response that we've  had to people who want to volunteer and  support the NHS I mean you know this is  a [ __ ] this morning the Europeans are  partial okay let me take your question  I'm not here to to dodge your questions  I just wanted to set out what we're  doing in terms of getting more  ventilators online as you said the  scheme that the the EU was was talking  about there was a miscommunication and  in fact the tendering process had  already started and what we've also been  very clear about is if there are any  future such schemes of course we will  look to participate in them but as the  EU itself has been very clear actually  every single country individually is  doing its bit to procure more  ventilators to make sure more  ventilators are built and that is what  the UK is doing as well but a ventilator  challenge ok the housing market falling  off a cliff according to the front page  of The Times this morning not lending  money don't sell your house you can  still put it on the market but you can't  move at the moment is that just one of  those casualties that we're just going  to have to come back to when we've got  the bandwidth well we've had some  guidance that the housing section has  put out this morning which is you know  perfectly sensible guidance on how  people should conduct themselves in this  market clearly there are people who have  got a mortgage who have exchanged we  have talked to the the multi lenders and  we have agreed that they would look to  extend those mortgages to a point at  which completion can take place we're  also talking to the conveyancing sector  to make sure that practically completion  dates can be moved forward but of course  people will understand that at this time  they should where it is at all possible  move their their dates for completion  but also move their their dates for  moving into new homes clearly where  someone is moving into an empty home or  there are particularly exceptional  circumstances or we say to people is  please observe the public health England  guidance when it comes to how you  interact with with other individuals but  you know okay  your listeners your viewers who will be  watching this will absolutely agree with  the guidance that we put out there which  is that at this time people should be  looking to delay any moves this is about  safety for them and safety for others in  our communities okay quick thought  before I let you go no we're out of time  I was a bit teary clapping for our  carers last night what did what about  you yeah absolutely I mean my family and  I we were in our driveway and I mean it  was really quite emotional because you  you heard this ripple of clapping and  applause down the street and this was  echoed in towns and cities across our  country the NHS is a fabulous  institution and we are providing all the  support we can to the NHS because at the  end of the day it is them and it's  delicious workers who are helping us get  through this very difficult time  no no I know as a key worker you have to  come out and leave the family at home  and we really do appreciate you taking  the time I hope you and your family keep  well thank you thank you very much

